\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}
\input{macros}

\title{Structure of the Kvanndalen Fold Belt: Traverse Notes}
\author{H.~Gr{\o}nvik and T.~Aasheim}
\date{Field Season 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Traverse A: Western Limb}

Bedding on the western limb is remarkably consistent along the
two kilometre front. The flagship outcrop at the road cut reads
$\strike{045}/\dip{30}$, and the same panel repeats at the
stream section upstream, where the exposure is cleaner but the
access is worse.

\section{Traverse B: Hinge Zone}

Toward the hinge the orientation steepens and the readings
scatter. The cliff band above the scree gives
$\strike{120}/\dip{40}$, while the fresh breakage surfaces fifty
metres below record $\strike{075}/\dip{25}$. Both stations sit
inside the mapped hinge corridor, so the spread is real and not
a measurement artefact.

\section{Traverse C: Eastern Limb}

The eastern limb overturns gradually as the traverse descends
toward the lake. The last readable panel, flagged with orange
tape, shows $\strike{160}/\dip{55}$ before the cover thickens
and the ridge loses outcrop entirely.

Foliation labels follow the \foliation{S-fabric} convention of
the report series, and younging evidence is tagged
\younging{2} throughout the notebook.

\end{document}

% Field notation macros --- structural geology report series
% MIGRATION NOTE: bedding pairs still use \strike/\dip; switch
% to the consolidated \plane form once drafting signs off (FG-118).
\newcommand{\strike}[1]{#1^{\circ}}
\newcommand{\dip}[1]{#1^{\circ}}
\newcommand{\foliation}[1]{\textit{#1}}
\newcommand{\younging}[1]{\textbf{Y#1}}

